    "body": "Great on any Friday: Bluetooth-FM Transmitter for Car - AUX input + 2x Charging ports (USB-C & USB-A)\nFord's Valencia Plant in Spain Is Becoming Part of Geely's European Strategy\n10 photos\nPhoto: Geely\nEurope's automotive industry has previously ignored Chinese newcomers. Things changed dramatically once those Chinese automakers started taking market share for themselves across the continent that gave us the likes of Volkswagen, Fiat, Citroen, and Volvo.\nAs far as Geely is concerned, the manufacturer's latest move can be summed up like this: why keep importing vehicles from China when you can use European factories controlled by your European competition?\nMichael Yang, the managing director of Geely Auto UK, told Automotive News that a new electric vehicle architecture will be localized at Ford 's plant in Valencia, Spain. Production should kick off in 2028. While Yang did not identify said platform or which models would use it, his announcement speaks volumes.\nTo bypass steep EU import tariffs on battery-electric vehicles and avoid caps on hybrid imports from China, both Leapmotor and BYD decided on building cars inside Europe. The lesser Chinese automaker took the fastest route, partnering with Stellantis to build the B10 crossover at the Zaragoza plant in Spain.\nBecause said facility takes care of stamping, welding, and painting with over 40 percent local content, Leapmotor's Stellantis-built vehicles qualify as made in Europe, thus dodging import duties. BYD, on the other hand, chose to build its own plant from the ground up in Szeged, Hungary. As if that wasn't enough, BYD is also planning to acquire Ineos Automotive's Hambach facility in France.\nPer available information, Ford will own 66 percent of the proposed joint venture with Geely. This arrangement gives the Chinese manufacturer access to an established vehicle assembly operation while the Dearborn-based partner fills capacity, spreading the cost of running such a major production complex. The Valencia site has the potential to make around 500,000 vehicles per annum.\nPhoto: Geely Considering that core Geely models for Europe are presently based on the Global Intelligent Electric Architecture and its Evo upgrade, the GEA Evo could very well be the multi-energy platform intended for the Valencia plant. In any case, Geely wants to sell 16 models in the European Union by 2028.\nYang also made it clear that Geely, which owns Volvo, intends to use Volvo's European manufacturing network to hit its 16-model target by 2028. Additions include a city car, smaller crossovers, and larger utility vehicles, including a full-size model. Just like Leapmotor and BYD, local production is the name of the game here. The attraction is terribly simple to understand.\nChina is already oversaturated, while European automakers continue to ignore European demand for cheap-and-cheerful vehicles. Even after accounting for tariffs and quotas, Chinese automakers manage to undercut European rivals, with price being key to the popularity of Chinese brands in said part of the world.\nBuilding locally would help Geely shorten the supply chain, save money using European industrial capacity, and put Geely closer to European dealer networks. Having a manufacturing operation in Europe can also change how the customer perceives a given vehicle, and that's really bad news for the establishment.\nEven though interests overlap, neither side pretends that this is an altruistic situation. Europe and its automakers need factories humming, workers working, and investments to keep things moving in the right direction. Chinese manufacturers need access without absorbing trade restrictions along the way.\nVia // Automotive News\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nGeely Ford Europe Spain production\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Square-Body Chevy Silverado Sat 15 Years Untouched, Rescuers Have 72 Hours To Make It Great Again",
